Assertions:
[BODY] withPrices = false on the order returned to the denied-role caller {SPEC} PR body — OrderOperation.WithPrices
[BODY] every field the PR body's Breaking Changes section names as newly zeroed reads 0/null for the denied-role caller: Total, SubTotal, SubTotalWithTax, DiscountAmount, DiscountTotal, DiscountTotalWithTax, Fee, FeeTotal, FeeTotalWithTax, FeeWithTax, HandlingTotal, HandlingTotalWithTax, OrderTotals (null), PaymentDiscountTotal, PaymentDiscountTotalWithTax, PaymentSubTotal, PaymentSubTotalWithTax, PaymentTaxTotal, PaymentTotal, PaymentTotalWithTax, ShippingDiscountTotal, ShippingDiscountTotalWithTax, ShippingSubTotal, ShippingSubTotalWithTax, ShippingTaxTotal, ShippingTotal, ShippingTotalWithTax, SubTotalDiscount, SubTotalDiscountWithTax, SubTotalTaxTotal, TaxPercentRate, TaxTotal {SPEC} PR body §Breaking changes, diff CustomerOrder.cs
[BODY] the SAME order read by {{ADMIN_EMAIL}} (permitted) in the comparison call shows every one of those 30 fields at the TRUE non-zero value captured in Preconditions — the zeroing is permission-driven, not a data gap {SPEC}

[BODY] every order returned to the denied-role caller has Total=0 and withPrices=false — redaction still applies to the results themselves {SPEC}
[BODY] the ascending-sort sequence of order numbers returned to the denied caller is NOT equal to the TRUE total-ascending ranking recorded in Preconditions {DOC} arch doc §Open questions "Indexed search leaks through filters and sorts" + {BL} — equality here IS the S4/ORDA-107 leak (the ordering discloses the hidden ranking even though every Total on the page reads 0); non-equality, or the endpoint refusing a price-based sort/filter for a denied caller outright, is the only way this passes
[BODY] the descending-sort sequence is likewise independent of the TRUE ranking, not merely the reverse of the ascending result {DOC}/{BL} — same predicate applied to the reverse direction

[BODY] the result set contains ZERO orders, OR contains only orders from stores within the caller's allowed scope ({{STORE_ID}}) — it must NEVER contain an order whose storeId is {{STORE_ID_SECONDARY}} or any store outside the allowed scope {SPEC} diff OrderAuthorizationHandler.cs — AllowedStoreIds.Intersect(criteria.StoreIds) can yield [] + {BL}
[STATUS] HTTP 200 is acceptable only if the result set satisfies the assertion above — a 200 carrying out-of-scope orders is the defect, not a pass {BL}
[BODY] the differently-cased out-of-scope request produces the identical (empty/in-scope-only) result — case-insensitive store matching must not create a bypass {SPEC} diff — DistinctIgnoreCase/ContainsIgnoreCase on store matching

[BODY] after the denied caller's PUT, the order re-read as the permitted admin shows Total and the sampled LineItem.Price EQUAL to the values captured before the walk — the price-blind save did not persist the zeros {SPEC} PR body "Prices are restored on every save... create, update, patch" + arch doc rule #2 + {BL}
[BODY] the non-price field the denied caller changed (OperatorComment) IS persisted — restore-on-write must not also revert the caller's legitimate edit {SPEC}
[BODY] the same two checks hold after the PATCH path {SPEC}
[STATUS] both the PUT and the PATCH return their normal success status (200/204), not an error — restore is transparent to the caller {SPEC}

[BODY] the exported CustomerOrders JSON contains the REAL non-zero Total/prices for every order in range, OR the job fails with an explicit, surfaced error naming the reason — never a silent Succeeded with every price zeroed {DOC} arch doc §The principal trap — "the correct default is full access, opt in" for a system context + {BL}
[STATE] if the job succeeded, compare its exported Total for one order against a privileged live GET of the same order {HYPOTHESIS} verify whether the current build surfaces ANY distinguishing signal (job status, log entry, notification) when a system-context export silently zeroes prices, since none is known to exist per the architecture doc
[REL] exported.Total for the sampled order equals privileged.Total from a live GET of the same order — exported.Total == privileged.Total and != 0. A Succeeded job whose exported.Total = 0 while privileged.Total != 0 is the defect {SPEC} PR body: export now routes through the protection service

[GRID] the list-grid price cell shows a real value for the entitled user, a masked state (blank/dash, never a value indistinguishable from a real 0) for the denied user, and a real value for the custom-rule user if applicable {SPEC} diff customerOrder-list.tpl.html — showPrice:row.entity.withPrices filter
[BLADE] the order-totals widget, line items, shipment widget, invoice, and operation-tree widget each mirror the SAME masked/unmasked state as the list grid for each user — no surface disagrees with another for the same user {SPEC} diff — all ten blade/template files were switched from authService.checkPermission('order:read_prices') to reading blade.<x>.withPrices in this PR; a missed file is a partial migration
[BLADE] the custom-rule user is shown real prices despite lacking the global order:read_prices permission — proving the UI reads the server's withPrices flag rather than re-deriving the rule client-side {SPEC} PR body "read that flag instead of re-deriving the rule from client-side permission checks" + {BL}

Steps:
[SETUP] identify the most reachable proxy for a clone:false / cache-owned read: read the SAME order as the denied user, then immediately (within the platform's normal order response-cache window) as the entitled user, via the standard GET routes
[AUTH] as the denied-role admin user: GET {{BACK_URL}}api/order/customerOrders/{id}
[AUTH] immediately after, as {{ADMIN_EMAIL}} (permitted): GET {{BACK_URL}}api/order/customerOrders/{id}
[CAPTURE] Total and one LineItem.Price from the second (entitled) response
[HTTP] repeat the same read pair for the order's shipment and payment sub-objects

Assertions:
[BODY] the entitled admin's read, taken immediately after the denied admin's read of the SAME order, shows the TRUE non-zero Total and LineItem.Price — not the zeros the denied caller received {SPEC} PR body "Also fixes CustomerOrder.Clone()... mutating a clone reached back into the original" + arch doc rule #1 + {BL}
[BODY] the same holds for the nested shipment and payment values on the entitled admin's read {SPEC}
[REL] the entitled admin's Total read AFTER the denied admin's read equals the entitled admin's Total captured BEFORE it — before vs after unchanged, and != 0. Any drift to 0 means the denied read poisoned the shared cache {SPEC} arch doc rule #1

Steps:
[SETUP] Confirm the disposable order from ORDA-104's fixture carries >=1 order-level discount, and where possible non-empty taxDetails/feeDetails, on the order AND on a line item AND on a shipment
[AUTH] as {{ADMIN_EMAIL}} (permitted): GET {{BACK_URL}}api/order/customerOrders/{id} → capture the TRUE order.discounts[], order.taxDetails[], order.feeDetails[], one lineItem.discounts[], and one shipment.discounts[]
[AUTH] as the denied-role admin user: GET {{BACK_URL}}api/order/customerOrders/{id}

Assertions:
[BODY] order.discounts[] on the denied caller's response is EMPTY or null — NOT the same non-empty array the permitted read captured {SPEC} diff CustomerOrderDataProtectionService.cs — RemovePrices calls ReduceDetails(Full & ~WithPrices), which clears only the WithPrices flag, leaving WithDiscounts set, so Discounts is never nulled — the concrete leak + {DOC} arch doc §Open questions #1 + {BL}
[BODY] order.taxDetails[] and order.feeDetails[] on the denied caller's response are EMPTY/null or otherwise carry no amount — ReduceDetails never references TaxDetails/FeeDetails at all per the diff, so they are untouched by construction {SPEC}/{BL}
[BODY] the same holds one level down: the sampled lineItem.discounts[] and the sampled shipment.discounts[] are empty/null for the denied caller, not the non-empty arrays the permitted read captured {SPEC}/{BL}
[MATH] the denied caller cannot reconstruct the withheld order.Total from order.discounts[].discountAmount plus any other surviving field — verify that whatever discount/total data IS present is insufficient to derive the true Total; per the live CO260909-00002 pattern, a bare discounts[0].discountAmount beside Total=0 already fails this check if Discounts is not nulled {DOC}/{BL}

Steps:
[SETUP] Take the price-free export produced by ORDA-112 (system-context export); DO NOT import it over live orders — import only into a scratch/disposable target (fresh orders with new ids, or a dedicated non-prod environment)
[NAV] {{ADMIN_URL}}
[ACT] sign in as {{ADMIN_EMAIL}}
[ACT] trigger Import using the ORDA-112 export file, targeting the disposable scratch orders
[WAIT] the import job reaches a terminal state (Succeeded/Failed)
[HTTP] as {{ADMIN_EMAIL}} (permitted): GET {{BACK_URL}}api/order/customerOrders/{imported order id} for one of the newly-created orders

Assertions:
[BODY] EITHER the import is refused/fails loudly (job status Failed with an explicit error identifying price-free/redacted source data), OR the imported order's Total and price fields match the ORIGINAL pre-export TRUE values (not the zeros from the price-free export) {DOC} arch doc rule #2 "a new entity has no stored counterpart and needs no restore" — combined with a system-context export that already denied prices, the round trip has no original to restore from, so a silent Succeeded-with-zeros is the defect + {BL}
[STATE] if the import succeeded, verify SOME signal (job warning, notification, or refusal) marks the imported prices as untrustworthy rather than treating the zero as ordinary data {HYPOTHESIS} verify whether any such signal exists in the current build; none is known to exist per the architecture doc
[REL] the imported order's Total equals the ORIGINAL pre-export Total captured privileged — imported.Total == original.Total and != 0. imported.Total = 0 against a non-zero original is silent data destruction {DOC} arch doc rule #2

[BLADE] the blade shows a scoped error/empty state for the order-totals section — never a whole-page crash, and never a fallback that renders a cached/stale REAL price when the fresh request failed {BL}
[BLADE] no numeric price value is rendered when the underlying request failed or returned malformed data — masking fails CLOSED (defaults to hidden), not open {SPEC}/{BL}
[BLADE] the count of currency-formatted values rendered anywhere in the order-totals section = 0 while the order request returns 500 / malformed JSON — masking fails CLOSED, and no stale real price is served from cache {BL} BL-UI-004
